WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.583
The archive runs four workers, each measured to
handle 100 requests per second for a specified

00:00:06.583 --> 00:00:07.213
workload.

00:00:07.213 --> 00:00:12.885
At 280 requests per second, removing one worker
leaves a nominal capacity of 300.

00:00:12.885 --> 00:00:18.628
That small margin may be inadequate once request
costs vary or a dependency slows.

00:00:18.628 --> 00:00:24.580
A rolling deployment needs spare capacity as well as
a sequence of replacement steps.

00:00:24.600 --> 00:00:31.039
Stop new assignments, allow existing work to drain
within a deadline, deploy, and verify readiness

00:00:31.039 --> 00:00:32.879
before returning the worker.

00:00:32.879 --> 00:00:39.383
A failed health probe is evidence that the checked
path did not respond acceptably; it is not proof

00:00:39.383 --> 00:00:40.632
of a process crash.

00:00:40.632 --> 00:00:45.559
Overload, network trouble or a dependency can
produce the same observation.

00:00:45.600 --> 00:00:52.035
Failure thresholds reduce reactions to brief
disturbances but delay removal, so some requests may

00:00:52.035 --> 00:00:53.893
fail before routing changes.

00:00:53.893 --> 00:00:57.276
Explain what clients may retry and with what limit.

00:00:57.276 --> 00:01:03.645
During a rolling update, old and new versions
coexist, which makes API and storage compatibility

00:01:03.645 --> 00:01:07.824
part of deployment safety rather than an optional
cleanup task.
